th low blood pressure (90/48)", "Unilateral purulent nasal discharge mixed with blood", "Fever unresponsive to standard decongestant therapy", "Progression after home treatment suggesting worsening infection"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Foreign Body Obstruction of Nasal Cavity", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.03202397129358178, "danger": 0.7, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Trauma absence provides moderate exclusion against foreign body (B1), though persistent unilateral symptoms maintain some support for structural/vascular causes (B3.1) without strong LR evidence for either."]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Acute Bacterial Sinusitis with Complication Pattern", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 3.21904774577178e-05, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Localized inflammation from vascular causes (B3.1) better explains tachycardia without systemic signs, providing weak against (LR- 1.726) for B2 while maintaining safety for B3.1."]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Anatomical/Nasal Structural Cause with Hemorrhagic Component", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.3549203579521559, "posterior": 0.5260105448078408, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["Normal respiratory findings (despite asthma history) and tachycardia favor an anatomical bleeding focus over systemic infection", "Unilateral purulent-bloody discharge more plausibly represents a specific anatomical complication rather than symmetrical bacterial sinusitis"], "evidence_against": ["The double worsening (worsening symptoms after 5 days despite initial treatment) supports bacterial sinusitis progression as per clinical guidance", "Child's age and no trauma history make spontaneous structural causes like septal perforation improbable, significantly reducing B3/B5 plausibility"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B3.1", "B3.2", "B3.3"], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of recurrent epistaxis", "Presence of septal deformity", "Trauma history in family"], "requestable_discriminators": ["NOSE CT with contrast", "Digital nasal imaging", "Anterior rhinoscopy"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.4, "interrupt_relevance": 0.3, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "etiology"}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Viral Upper Respiratory Infection with Secondary Hemorrhage", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.11934874597139496, "danger": 0.2, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Nasal/Oronasal Malignancy with Inflammatory Pattern", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.024863369220127773, "danger": 0.9, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B3.1": {"id": "B3.1", "label": "Acute Vascular Lesion of the Nasal Cavity with Hemorrhage", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.3436717100444289, "posterior": 0.5125548820625226, "danger": 0.7, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Unilateral purulent-hemorrhagic discharge with localized fever weakens bacterial sinusitis (B2, LR- 1.5547 for viral) while supporting localized vascular causes (B3.1) through pattern analysis, though statistical LR thresholds prevent stronger assignment.", "Localized inflammation from vascular causes (B3.1) better explains tachycardia without systemic signs, providing weak against (LR- 1.726) for B2 while maintaining safety for B3.1."], "evidence_against": ["Child's age and trauma absence reduce likelihood of spontaneous vascular lesion (B3.1) but leave room for acquired causes or foreign body (B1) reconsideration.", "Absence of trauma provides moderate against (LR- 0.5214) for B3.1's spontaneity, but the hemorrhagic pattern still supports acquired vascular changes as part of the anatomical family (B3)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["History of recent unilateral nasal trauma or epistaxis", "Evidence of rapidly growing mass"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Nasal endoscopy with Doppler imaging", "Contrast-enhanced MRI of nasal vault"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.2, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "anatomy"}, "B3.2": {"id": "B3.2", "label": "Chronic Anatomical Narrowing with Inflammatory Complications", "level": 2, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.001277664212878498, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "B3", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["No documented trauma history provides moderate evidence against foreign body obstruction (B1) but does not completely exclude it."]}, "B3.3": {"id": "B3.3", "label": "Spontaneous Septal Perforation with Superimposed Infection", "parent": "B3", "level": 2, "status": "reopened", "prior": 0.009725514610747341, "posterior": 0.012177998532439754, "danger": 0.4, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Absence of trauma provides moderate against (LR- 0.5214) for B3.1's spontaneity, but the hemorrhagic pattern still supports acquired vascular changes as part of the anatomical family (B3)."], "evidence_against": ["Controlled asthma with albuterol alone is unlikely to cause sufficient mucosal fragility for spontaneous septal perforation in a pediatric patient.", "While trauma history is atypical for septal perforation in children, spontaneous perforation from aggressive infection (not trauma) remains a possible explanation."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "reopen_triggers": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Prior history of rhinoplasty or nasal injury", "Unilateral crusting or anterior nasal discomfort"], "requestable_discriminators": ["Anterior rhinoscopy for septal defect", "Nasal swab culture for infective agent"], "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.2, "interrupt_relevance": 0.1, "level_role": "specific_disease", "classification_axis": "etiology"}}, "frontier": ["B3.1", "B3.1", "B3.3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 0, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the unilateral purulent-bloody discharge in a pediatric patient receiving standard decongestant therapy and lacking trauma history strong enough to support foreign body obstruction (B1) over other anatomical causes?", "result_summary": "Unilateral purulent-bloody discharge raises suspicion for foreign body but is tempered by absence of trauma history, leaving it neutral for B2 while moderate against B1 due to the lack of object insertion documentation.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B3.1": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 1, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the child's age (7 years) and progressive unilateral purulent discharge with pain after decongestant failure argue against viral upper respiratory infection and support bacterial sinusitis (B2)?", "result_summary": "The low-grade fever and unilateral symptoms are atypical for bacterial sinusitis (B2), with LR evidence showing weak reduction in B2 probability due to absence of systemic toxicity, while structural causes remain plausible.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "weak_against", "B3": "neutral", "B3.1": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 2, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the unilateral purulent-bloody discharge mixed with persistent localized fever (37.5°C) more consistent with Kiesselbach plexus vascular involvement (B3.1) or typical bacterial superinfection (B2)?", "result_summary": "Unilateral purulent-hemorrhagic discharge with localized fever weakens bacterial sinusitis (B2, LR- 1.5547 for viral) while supporting localized vascular causes (B3.1) through pattern analysis, though statistical LR thresholds prevent stronger assignment.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "moderate_against", "B3": "neutral", "B3.1": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 3, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of documented nasal trauma or object insertion in this 7-year-old child provide sufficient safety exclusion for a foreign body obstruction (B1) despite unilateral purulent-hemorrhagic discharge?", "result_summary": "Trauma absence provides moderate exclusion against foreign body (B1), though persistent unilateral symptoms maintain some support for structural/vascular causes (B3.1) without strong LR evidence for either.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "moderate_against", "B2": "neutral", "B3": "neutral", "B3.1": "neutral"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 4, "bundle_size": 6, "action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "external_action": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of systemic toxicity despite persistent tachycardia (124bpm) and low-grade fever (99.5°F) disqualify bacterial sinusitis (B2) in favor of structural/vascular alternatives?", "result_summary": "Localized inflammation from vascular causes (B3.1) better explains tachycardia without systemic signs, providing weak against (LR- 1.726) for B2 while maintaining safety for B3.1.", "per_action_branch_effects": {"B1": "neutral", "B2": "weak_against", "B3": "neutral", "B3.1": "weak_for"}}, {"timestep": 5, "bundle_id": 5, "bundle_position": 5, "bundle_size": 6, "action